<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    The widget picker's preview of the grid widget.

    Static and complete: the picker never asks the app for anything, so a placed
    widget, an app update and a reboot leave it exactly as it is (ADR 37). It is
    also only resources - shape drawables, exported vector glyphs and colours
    with a values-night variant - so it is correct by construction and costs
    nothing to keep so. Nothing here is read from the database or rasterized at
    runtime.

    What it draws is the real wide grid at its two-row size: the same selector,
    the same 44dp tiles at the same corner ratio, the same 16% wash under the
    glyph, and the first seven categories of the default seed with their own
    icons, colours and names. The previous version stood in for all of that with
    eight blank rounded rectangles, which read as a widget still loading rather
    than as the widget the user is about to place.

    The wash is android:alpha over the surface rather than a second set of
    colours: the surface is a dynamic system token, so there is no build-time
    value to pre-blend against - and unlike the placed widget, a static layout
    can simply carry view alpha.

    Only views RemoteViews accepts (LinearLayout, FrameLayout, TextView,
    ImageView): the host inflates this the same way it inflates a widget, and
    anything richer has already proved unreliable here once (adaptive icons
    rendered as a full squircle on one button and as nothing on the other).

    Colours come from res/values(-night)/colors.xml, never from ?android:attr: a
    previewLayout is inflated with a plain application context whose theme falls
    back to Theme.DeviceDefault.Light, so theme attributes gave near-black ink on
    a dark picker sheet.
-->
<LinearLayout xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    android:background="@drawable/widget_preview_background"
